  "lesson.intro.title": "Welcome to 3D: planes & axes",
  "lesson.intro.intro": "Explore one idea at a time. The 3D view highlights what we are talking about.",
  "lesson.intro.intro.simple": "Read the card, find the bright shape, then continue when you are ready.",
  "lesson.intro.steps": ["<p>An <b>axis</b> is a straight reference line that gives us a direction in 3D.</p><p>Find the bright <b>red X axis</b>. Its arrow points toward positive X. The opposite direction is negative X.</p><p>We often measure a part’s width along X. The other two axes are faded so you can focus on this one.</p>", "<p>Now find the bright <b>green Y axis</b>. It gives us a second direction, at a right angle to X.</p><p>We often measure depth along Y. Follow the green arrow to see positive Y.</p>", "<p>The bright <b>blue Z axis</b> gives us the third direction: height.</p><p>Its arrow points upward, toward positive Z. X, Y, and Z together let us describe positions in 3D.</p>", "<p>Look at the <b>orange point where all three axes meet</b>. This is the <b>origin</b>: our shared starting point for measurements.</p><p>Its coordinates are <b>(0, 0, 0)</b>: zero along X, zero along Y, and zero along Z.</p><p>A point at (10, 0, 0) is 10 millimeters from here along the red X arrow. All lengths in this app are in millimeters (mm).</p>", "<p>A <b>plane</b> is a flat surface that extends in two directions. We show just a small patch so you can see it.</p><p>The highlighted <b>XY plane</b> lies along the red X and green Y axes, like a floor. Everywhere on it, Z is zero.</p><p>A sketch on this plane is a flat drawing with X and Y coordinates.</p>", "<p>The highlighted <b>XZ plane</b> lies along the red X and blue Z axes, like a wall.</p><p>Everywhere on it, Y is zero. A sketch here can describe a part’s width and height.</p><p>The plane’s name tells you which two axes it uses.</p>", "<p>The highlighted <b>YZ plane</b> lies along the green Y and blue Z axes, like the side wall.</p><p>Everywhere on it, X is zero. A sketch here can describe depth and height.</p><p>All three origin planes pass through the origin. Next, try looking around and choosing a sketch plane yourself.</p>", "Find the <b>view cube</b> at the bottom right. Click <b>Top</b> to look straight down the Z axis onto the XY plane. Moving the camera changes your view, not your design.", "Click an <b>edge</b> of the view cube to look between two faces. Corners show three sides at once. You can also drag the empty view to orbit, scroll to zoom, and Shift-drag to pan. The house button returns to the home view.", "Click <b>Create Sketch</b>, then the <b>XY plane</b> (the floor). A sketch has only two directions: here X and Y. The camera looks straight at it so you can draw accurately.", "Click <b>Finish Sketch</b>. An empty sketch is discarded. Click <b>Create Sketch</b> again and choose <b>XZ</b> (the back wall). Here sketch height follows Z, the blue axis.", "Click <b>Finish Sketch</b>, then <b>Create Sketch</b> and choose <b>YZ</b> (the side wall). The plane you choose sets the orientation of your drawing in 3D.", "Click <b>Finish Sketch</b> to return to 3D. You have explored all three origin planes!"],
  "lesson.intro.outro": "You can now navigate a 3D workspace and choose a sketch plane. Next, draw a 2D rectangle and extrude it to make a 3D solid. Faces are surfaces of that solid; edges are where its faces meet; vertices are where edges meet.",
  "lesson.intro.outro.simple": "You know the room, the rulers, and how to look around. Next you will draw a rectangle on the floor and pull it up into a block!",
